-- title:
--   Residue ring at π is algebraically closed of characteristic r
-- statement:
--   Let $r$ be a prime number, let $\mathcal O$ be a commutative ring and $\pi \in \mathcal O$ an element generating the same ideal as the image of $r$, that is, $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$. Let $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ be a commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra, and write $\mathfrak p = (\,\text{the image of }\pi\,)$ for the ideal of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ generated by the image of $\pi$ under the structure map $\mathcal O \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$. Assume $\mathfrak p$ is a maximal ideal of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$, and assume that for every monic polynomial $p \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}[X]$ of positive degree there exists $x \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ with $p(x) \in \mathfrak p$. Then the quotient $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/\mathfrak p$, equipped with the field structure arising from maximality of $\mathfrak p$, is algebraically closed, and it has characteristic $r$.
--
--   This is the basic statement that the residue ring of the ring of unramified coefficients is an algebraically closed field of characteristic $r$, extracting from the axiomatic hypotheses ($\pi$ generating $(r)$, maximality of $(\pi)$, roots of monic polynomials modulo $\pi$) the properties of the residue field used throughout the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation frame. It is invoked by the statements about fake elliptic curves and their rigidifications, isogeny pairs and dual germs, where the residue field is required to be algebraically closed.
-- source:
